module

public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.UpperShriek.Composition
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.UpperShriek.DualityFormula
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.UpperShriek.PolynomialEvalDualityProof

/-!
# The surjective case of algebraic upper shriek at an arbitrary presentation

Let `φ : R →+* A` be a surjective ring homomorphism.  The statements this library proves about
`φ^!` for such a `φ` are all proved at the one presentation a surjection carries for free, the
one by the polynomial algebra in *no* variables, because that is where derived coextension of
scalars appears with no shift and no base change to move past it.  A general presentation of `φ`
has `n` variables, and the passage from `n = 0` to general `n` is exactly the independence of the
presentation, [Stacks, Tag 0BZJ].

That independence is unconditional in this library:
`Dualizing.PolynomialPresentation.nonempty_upperShriekFunctor_iso`, whose hypothesis
`Dualizing.PolynomialEvalDuality` is discharged by `Dualizing.polynomialEvalDuality`.  This file
makes the passage once and reads off the consequences, so that the restriction *at its
presentation with no variables* disappears from the statements that carried it.

## Main results

* `Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_iso_derivedHom_of_surjective` — **[Stacks, Tag 0C0G] for a
  surjective `φ` at an arbitrary polynomial presentation of `φ`**: `φ^!` is derived coextension
  of scalars `RHom_R(A, -)`, at every presentation and not only at the one with no variables.
  This is the passage; everything else here is an application of it.
* `Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_comp_of_surjective` — **[Stacks, Tag 0BZT] for a surjective
  second map, at arbitrary presentations** of the first map, of the second and of the composite.
* `Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_natIso_dualizingDerivedHom_of_surjective` — **[Stacks, Tag 0E9L]
  for a surjective `φ` at an arbitrary presentation**, as an isomorphism of functors on
  `D_Coh(A)`.  The general finite-type case of that tag is
  `Dualizing.PolynomialPresentation.nonempty_upperShriekFunctor_natIso_dualizingDerivedHom` of
  `StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.UpperShriek.FiniteTypeDualityFormula`, which is also
  unconditional; it does not go through the composition isomorphism of [Stacks, Tag 0BZT], so the
  remarks below about Tag 0BZT in general do not apply to it.

The same passage removes the restriction from [Stacks, Tag 0E9R] too; that is in
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.UpperShriek.RelativeDualizingVanishingAnyPresentation`, kept
apart because of the derived-category model (see the implementation notes).

## Implementation notes

The hypothesis of surjectivity is *not* removed by this argument, which generalises in the
presentations only; it is removed in
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.UpperShriek.Finite`, where the statement below is the
surjective half of the reduction.  Tag 0C0G is about a finite `φ`, and the source's reduction of
the finite case to a surjection goes through an intermediate
`C = R[x_1, …, x_n]/(f_1(x_1), …, f_n(x_n))` with the `f_i` monic.  The self-duality
`Hom_R(C, R) ≅ C` — the source's generator `δ_{d-1}` — is `Dualizing.adjoinRootSelfDualIso` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.PowerBasisSelfDual` composed along the tower by
`Dualizing.IsSelfDualAlgebra.trans`, and its derived form `RHom_R(C, -) ≅ - ⊗_R^L C`, which is
[Stacks, Tag 0BZE] read at a self-dual finite projective algebra, is
`Dualizing.nonempty_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensorProductOver_of_selfDual_algebra` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.SelfDualDerivedTensor`; the factorization of a finite `φ` as a
surjection onto `A` from such a `C` is `Dualizing.exists_monicIdeal_quotient_surjection` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.MonicQuotient` (`Dualizing.nonempty_monicTowerFactorization` is
the same factorization with `C` built as an iterated `AdjoinRoot` rather than as a quotient of a
polynomial ring).

The value of upper shriek of the bottom map on the base,
`ω_{C/R} = (R → C)^!(R) ≅ C` in degree zero, is
`Dualizing.nonempty_upperShriekFunctor_obj_unit_iso_single_monicIdeal`: Tag 0BZU and Tag 0E4B
reduce `(R → C)^!` to derived tensoring with that object and
`Dualizing.PolynomialPresentation.nonempty_upperShriekFunctor_obj_unit_iso_single` computes it,
both asking for the presentation of `R → C` by `n` variables to have its kernel generated by a
weakly regular sequence of length `n`, namely `f_1(x_1), …, f_n(x_n)`, which is
`MvPolynomial.isWeaklyRegular_ofFn_aeval_X_of_monic`.

Tag 0BZT is generalised here in the presentations only, not in the hypothesis on the second map.
The general case needs the flat base change comparison of [Stacks, Tag 0BZM] to move the second
presentation's variables past the first `RHom`; it is
`Dualizing.PolynomialPresentation.exists_upperShriekComp_of_finiteType` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Dualizing.UpperShriek.CompositionFiniteType`, which carries the tag; it
asks for `R` Noetherian, as the source does, and gives an isomorphism only on `D⁺(R)`.  A
surjection has no variables to move, which is why the surjective case below needs no Noetherian
hypothesis and holds on all of `D(R)`.

The `HasDerivedCategory` instance of `Dualizing.IsDualizingComplex` is spelled out rather than
left to instance search.  Tag 0BZJ is proved in a module whose import closure contains the
global locally small instances of
`StacksFrontier.Homology.DerivedCategory.LocallySmall`, which instance search prefers to the
file-local `HasDerivedCategory.standard`; every other derived category in the statements below
is pinned by an explicit universe argument, and this one is pinned by name for the same reason.
`StacksFrontier.Homology.DerivedCategory.ModelComparisonTransport` explains the two models.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C0G](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C0G)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BZJ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BZJ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BZT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BZT)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0E9L](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E9L)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory

attribute [local instance] HasDerivedCategory.standard

universe u v

namespace Dualizing

noncomputable section

variable {R A B : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [CommRing B]

/-- **Stacks Project, Tag 0C0G, for a surjective `φ`, at an arbitrary polynomial presentation.**
Let `φ : R →+* A` be surjective and let `p` be any polynomial presentation of `φ`.  Then the
algebraic upper shriek functor of `p` is the derived coextension-of-scalars functor
`RHom_R(A, -) : D(R) ⥤ D(A)` of [Stacks, Tag 0A70].

`Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_ofSurjective_iso_derivedHom` is the case of the presentation with
no variables; the passage to an arbitrary presentation is the independence of the presentation,
[Stacks, Tag 0BZJ], which this library has unconditionally as
`Dualizing.PolynomialPresentation.nonempty_upperShriekFunctor_iso`.

The finite case of Tag 0C0G is `Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_iso_derivedHom_of_finite`, which
reduces to this statement; see the module docstring. -/
theorem upperShriekFunctor_iso_derivedHom_of_surjective
    (f : R →+* A) (hf : Function.Surjective f) (p : PolynomialPresentation f) :
    Nonempty (p.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v} ≅ derivedHom.{u, v, u} f) := by
  obtain ⟨eInd⟩ := PolynomialPresentation.nonempty_upperShriekFunctor_iso.{u, v} f p
    (PolynomialPresentation.ofSurjective f hf)
  obtain ⟨e⟩ := upperShriekFunctor_ofSurjective_iso_derivedHom.{u, v} f hf
  exact ⟨eInd ≪≫ e⟩

/-- **The composition law for upper shriek, for a surjective second map, at arbitrary
presentations.**  Let
`g : R →+* A` be of finite type with a polynomial presentation `p`, let `h : A →+* B` be
surjective with an arbitrary polynomial presentation `q`, and let `r` be an arbitrary polynomial
presentation of `h ∘ g`.  Then

`h^! ∘ g^! ≅ (h ∘ g)^!`

with upper shriek read at `p`, at `q` and at `r`.

`Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_comp_ofSurjective` is the case `q = ` the presentation of `h` with
no variables and `r = p.compSurjective h hh`; the passage to arbitrary `q` and `r` is
`Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_iso_derivedHom_of_surjective` for `q` and
[Stacks, Tag 0BZJ] for `r`.  As there, the isomorphism is on all of `D(R)` and not merely on
`D⁺(R)`. -/
theorem upperShriekFunctor_comp_of_surjective {g : R →+* A} (p : PolynomialPresentation g)
    {h : A →+* B} (hh : Function.Surjective h) (q : PolynomialPresentation h)
    (r : PolynomialPresentation (h.comp g)) :
    Nonempty (p.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v} ⋙ q.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v} ≅
      r.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v}) := by
  obtain ⟨eq⟩ := upperShriekFunctor_iso_derivedHom_of_surjective.{u, v} h hh q
  obtain ⟨eComp⟩ := p.upperShriekFunctor_comp_derivedHom.{u, v} h hh
  obtain ⟨eInd⟩ := PolynomialPresentation.nonempty_upperShriekFunctor_iso.{u, v} (h.comp g)
    (p.compSurjective h hh) r
  exact ⟨Functor.isoWhiskerLeft p.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v} eq ≪≫ eComp ≪≫ eInd⟩

/-- **[Stacks, Tag 0E9L] for a surjective `φ`, at an arbitrary polynomial presentation, as an
isomorphism of functors on `D_Coh(A)`.**  Let `φ : A →+* B` be a surjection of commutative rings
with `A` Noetherian, let `p` be any polynomial presentation of `φ` and let `ω_A` be a dualizing
complex for `A`.  Write `φ^!` for the algebraic upper shriek functor of `p`, put
`ω_B = φ^!(ω_A)`, `D_A(-) = RHom_A(-, ω_A)` and `D_B(-) = RHom_B(-, ω_B)`.  Then

`φ^!(K) ≅ D_B(D_A(K) ⊗_A^L B)`

as an isomorphism of functors on `D_Coh(A)`.

`Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_ofSurjective_natIso_dualizingDerivedHom` is the case of the
presentation with no variables; this is that statement read through
`Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_iso_derivedHom_of_surjective`, which identifies `φ^!` with derived
coextension of scalars at *every* presentation.  Note that `ω_B` moves with the presentation, so
the right-hand side is transported too, by
`Dualizing.dualizingBaseChangeFunctorIsoOfIso`. -/
def upperShriekFunctor_natIso_dualizingDerivedHom_of_surjective [IsNoetherianRing A]
    (φ : A →+* B) (hφ : Function.Surjective φ) (p : PolynomialPresentation φ)
    (E : CochainComplex.DerivedExtensionScalars.TotalDerivedExtensionScalars.{u, v, u,
      max u v + 1, max u v + 1} φ)
    (iStar : DerivedCategory (ModuleCat.{max u v} B) ⥤ DerivedCategory (ModuleCat.{max u v} A))
    (adj : iStar ⊣ derivedHom.{u, v, u} φ)
    (ωA : DerivedCategory (ModuleCat.{max u v} A))
    (hωA : @IsDualizingComplex A _ _ (HasDerivedCategory.standard _) ωA) :
    (hasFiniteCohomology.{u, v} A).ι ⋙ p.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v} ≅
      (hasFiniteCohomology.{u, v} A).ι ⋙ dualizingBaseChangeFunctor.{u, v} φ E ωA
        (p.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v}.obj ωA) :=
  let e := (upperShriekFunctor_iso_derivedHom_of_surjective.{u, v} φ hφ p).some
  Functor.isoWhiskerLeft _ e ≪≫
    derivedHomNatIsoDualizingDerivedHom.{u, v} φ E iStar adj
      (fun N F => (ProjectionFormula.derivedProjNatIso.{u, v} φ E iStar adj N).app F)
      (fun N {_ _} f =>
        (ProjectionFormula.derivedProjNatIso.{u, v} φ E iStar adj N).hom.naturality f)
      (fun {_ _} n F => NatTrans.congr_app
        ((ProjectionFormula.derivedProjBifunctorNatIso.{u, v} φ E iStar adj).hom.naturality n) F)
      ωA hωA ≪≫
    Functor.isoWhiskerLeft _
      (dualizingBaseChangeFunctorIsoOfIso.{u, v} φ E ωA (e.app ωA).symm)

/-- **[Stacks, Tag 0E9L] for a surjective `φ` at an arbitrary presentation**, at a single `K`.
This is the value at `K` of
`Dualizing.upperShriekFunctor_natIso_dualizingDerivedHom_of_surjective`, which carries the
tag. -/
def upperShriekFunctor_iso_dualizingDerivedHom_of_surjective [IsNoetherianRing A]
    (φ : A →+* B) (hφ : Function.Surjective φ) (p : PolynomialPresentation φ)
    (E : CochainComplex.DerivedExtensionScalars.TotalDerivedExtensionScalars.{u, v, u,
      max u v + 1, max u v + 1} φ)
    (iStar : DerivedCategory (ModuleCat.{max u v} B) ⥤ DerivedCategory (ModuleCat.{max u v} A))
    (adj : iStar ⊣ derivedHom.{u, v, u} φ)
    (ωA K : DerivedCategory (ModuleCat.{max u v} A))
    (hωA : @IsDualizingComplex A _ _ (HasDerivedCategory.standard _) ωA)
    (hK : ∀ i : ℤ, Module.Finite A
      ((DerivedCategory.homologyFunctor (ModuleCat.{max u v} A) i).obj K)) :
    p.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v}.obj K ≅
      (dualizingDerivedHomFunctor.{u, v} B (p.upperShriekFunctor.{u, v}.obj ωA)).obj
        (Opposite.op (E.functor.obj
          ((dualizingDerivedHomFunctor.{u, v} A ωA).obj (Opposite.op K)))) :=
  (upperShriekFunctor_natIso_dualizingDerivedHom_of_surjective.{u, v} φ hφ p E iStar adj
    ωA hωA).app ⟨K, hK⟩

end

end Dualizing

end
